## 4. Relations Without Prior Relata

A relational structure may be specified without assuming primitive objects.

At minimum, one may begin with relations and composition rules.

Let \(\mathcal{R}\) be a collection of directed relations.

Relations may be composed:

\[
r:s\to t,
\qquad
q:t\to u,
\qquad
q\circ r:s\to u.
\]

Composition is associative:

\[
h\circ(g\circ f)
=
(h\circ g)\circ f.
\]

From this relational structure, objects can be recovered as identity relations.

An identity relation \(e_s\) associated with a relatum \(s\) satisfies:

\[
e_s\circ e_s
=
e_s,
\]

and acts as a unit for compatible relations:

\[
r\circ e_s
=
r,
\qquad
e_t\circ r
=
r.
\]

Thus objects appear as idempotent fixed points of relational composition.

This is a precise sense in which relata are derived.

They are not added to the relational structure from outside.

They are the stable identity elements internal to it.

