## 5. Yoneda Perspective: Objects as Relational Profiles

Category theory gives the deepest formulation of relational ontology.

Let \(\mathbf{C}\) be a category.

For an object \(X\), define the representable functor,

\[
h_X
=
\mathrm{Hom}_{\mathbf{C}}(-,X)
:
\mathbf{C}^{op}
\to
\mathbf{Set}.
\]

This functor encodes all relations into \(X\).

It says:

\[
h_X(A)
=
\mathrm{Hom}(A,X).
\]

That is, the being of \(X\) is given by how every other object \(A\) relates to \(X\).

The Yoneda lemma states that for any functor \(F\),

\[
\mathrm{Nat}(h_X,F)
\cong
F(X).
\]

In particular,

\[
\mathrm{Nat}(h_X,h_Y)
\cong
\mathrm{Hom}(X,Y).
\]

Thus a morphism \(f:X\to Y\) corresponds naturally to a transformation between relational profiles.

If

\[
h_X
\cong
h_Y,
\]

then

\[
X
\cong
Y.
\]

Thus:

\[
\boxed{
X
\cong
\mathrm{Hom}(-,X).
}
\]

More precisely, \(X\) is determined up to isomorphism by its relational profile.

This is the mathematical core of Pure Relation / Relata Relativity.

