## 9. Spacetime Points as Relational Fixed Points

General relativity provides a decisive argument against primitive spacetime points.

The hole argument shows that if one redistributes fields over points by a diffeomorphism that is nontrivial only inside a hole, while leaving fields outside unchanged, the two mathematical models are physically indistinguishable.

If spacetime points had primitive identity, the two models would represent different physical possibilities.

But they do not.

Thus spacetime points lack haecceity.

They are not primitive substances.

They are relational positions determined by fields, causal structure, and metric relations.

A spacetime point is not a bare container.

It is a stable coincidence of relations.

Thus:

\[
\boxed{
\text{Spacetime points are fixed points of field-causal-metric relations.}
}
\]

---

## 10. Causal Sets and Relational Spacetime

In causal-set theory, spacetime is approximated by a locally finite partial order,

\[
(C,\prec).
\]

The fundamental relation is causal precedence:

\[
x\prec y.
\]

The order plus number principle states:

\[
\text{order}+\text{number}
\rightarrow
\text{geometry}.
\]

The elements of the causal set are not primitive spacetime points.

They are discrete relational events.

Their identity is given by their causal relations to other events.

Thus causal-set theory is a concrete realization of Pure Relation / Relata Relativity.

