## 12. Tensor Networks and Entanglement Relations

Tensor networks represent quantum states as relational contractions.

A tensor-network state has the form,

\[
\psi_{s_1\cdots s_N}
=
\sum_{\text{internal}}
\prod_v T_v.
\]

The network’s geometry is not fundamental.

It emerges from entanglement structure.

The Ryu–Takayanagi formula relates entanglement entropy to geometric area:

\[
S(A)
=
\frac{\operatorname{Area}(\gamma_A)}{4G_N\hbar}.
\]

Thus spatial connectivity is encoded in entanglement relations.

The nodes of the tensor network are not fundamental substances.

They are relational tensors whose meaning is fixed by contraction relations.

