## 15. Identity as Relational Profile

Identity is not primitive self-sameness.

Identity is relational invariance.

An object persists if its relational profile remains stable under transformations.

Let \(T\) be a transformation.

An object \(X\) persists under \(T\) if

\[
T(X)
\cong
X.
\]

More generally, persistence is not exact identity but continuity of relational role.

This is the relational solution to the problem of change.

An object changes while remaining the same because its relational profile remains stable enough.

Thus:

\[
\boxed{
\text{Persistence is stability of relational profile.}
}
\]

---

## 16. Dynamics as Transformation of Relations

If relations are primitive, what is dynamics?

Dynamics is transformation of relational structure.

Let \(\mathcal{R}_t\) be the relational structure at parameter \(t\).

Dynamics is a flow,

\[
\mathcal{R}_t
\to
\mathcal{R}_{t'}.
\]

Objects persist as invariant patterns within this flow.

Thus time does not require external objects moving.

Time may itself be relational change.

This connects to Leibniz, Mach, and relational mechanics.

