## Appendix A: Yoneda Lemma Proof Sketch

Let \(\mathbf{C}\) be locally small.

For objects \(X,Y\), define

\[
h_X
=
\mathrm{Hom}(-,X).
\]

A natural transformation

\[
\alpha:h_X\to h_Y
\]

assigns to each object \(A\) a function,

\[
\alpha_A:
\mathrm{Hom}(A,X)
\to
\mathrm{Hom}(A,Y),
\]

natural in \(A\).

Define

\[
f
=
\alpha_X(\mathrm{id}_X)
\in
\mathrm{Hom}(X,Y).
\]

For any \(g:A\to X\), naturality gives

\[
\alpha_A(g)
=
f\circ g.
\]

Thus \(\alpha\) is determined by \(f\).

Conversely, any \(f:X\to Y\) defines a natural transformation by composition.

Therefore,

\[
\mathrm{Nat}(h_X,h_Y)
\cong
\mathrm{Hom}(X,Y).
\]

If \(h_X\cong h_Y\), then \(X\cong Y\).

Thus objects are determined by relational profiles.

